      <h2 class="section__title">Manufacturing Platform vs Direct Factory FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is it cheaper to use a manufacturing platform or go direct?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Going direct to a clothing manufacturer is typically 15-30% cheaper than using a platform. Platforms add markup to factory prices to cover their services. On a $20,000 annual manufacturing spend, a 20% platform markup costs $4,000 per year | $12,000 over three years. That's the cost of an entire extra production run. The savings compound as your order volume increases, making direct relationships increasingly economical as you scale.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I find clothing factories without using a platform?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Find direct factories through industry associations like BGMEA for Bangladesh or BKMEA for knitwear manufacturers. Trade shows such as Texworld, MAGIC, and Premi - re Vision feature factory exhibitors. Google searches with specific terms like "clothing manufacturer Bangladesh" or "private label clothing manufacturer" yield direct results. Referrals from other brands in your network are valuable. Sourcing agents can also introduce you to factories, though they charge fees. Direct factory relationships require more upfront research but yield better long-term value.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the typical markup on manufacturing platforms?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Manufacturing platforms typically add 15-30% markup to factory prices. This margin covers their vetting services, customer support, and platform operations. The markup varies by platform and order size - smaller orders often face higher percentage markups because fixed platform costs are spread across fewer units. Some platforms charge flat fees in addition to percentage markups. Going direct eliminates this cost entirely, putting that margin back into your business for product development, marketing, or profit.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can small brands work directly with factories?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, small brands can work directly with factories. Many overseas manufacturers, including SDF Clothing, accept orders as low as 300 pieces per style. Direct factories often have more flexibility than platforms for small orders because they value building long-term relationships. The key is having clear specifications, professional communication, and realistic expectations. Platforms sometimes market lower MOQs by aggregating orders, but the per-unit cost with markup is often higher than direct factory pricing at the 300-piece level.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I know if a factory is reliable without a platform?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Verify factory reliability through certifications (GOTS, OEKO-TEX, BSCI, WRAP, ISO 9001), industry association membership (BGMEA, BKMEA), client references from brands similar to yours, factory audit reports from third parties like BSCI or WRAP, and sample quality evaluation. Request photos of similar past production. Check online reviews and industry reputation. Start with a small trial order before committing to larger production runs. Reliable factories are transparent about capabilities and willing to provide verification.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What happens if quality is bad when I work directly?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            When working directly, quality issues are resolved through direct communication with the factory. This is often faster than going through a platform because you speak directly with decision-makers who can authorize corrections. Protect yourself with clear contracts, written sample approvals before bulk production, and third-party quality inspection before final payment. Direct relationships give you more leverage to resolve issues because the factory values your long-term business. Platforms may be slower to resolve problems as they coordinate between you and the factory.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Do direct factories have minimum order requirements?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, direct factories have MOQs, but they vary widely. Many quality overseas manufacturers accept 300-500 piece minimums per style with flexible size and color combinations. Platforms sometimes market lower MOQs because they aggregate orders across multiple clients to meet factory minimums. However, the platform markup on small orders can be proportionally higher. Direct factories offer better value at the 300+ piece level because you're paying factory pricing without intermediary markup. As your volume grows, direct factories provide increasingly better terms.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is it risky to work with an overseas factory directly?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Working directly with overseas factories carries similar risks to working through a platform - the factory is the same either way. The key is due diligence: verify certifications, check references, use contracts and NDAs, and start with small orders. Direct communication actually reduces risk because you build a relationship with the actual production team, not a middleman. You control the relationship directly rather than relying on a platform's interpretation of issues. With proper vetting and clear agreements, direct factory relationships are no riskier than platform-mediated ones.
          </p>
        </div>
